// HAND PATCH support: some pool allocation strategies in this file (traced
// via live memory inspection) can hand back a base address sitting right at
// or inside a read-only guard page ReXGlue places between memory regions --
// a genuinely-mapped page (so mincore() sees it as resident) that just isn't
// writable, causing writes into the "allocated" chunk to SIGSEGV.
//
// HAND PATCH FIX: this used to pre-check /proc/self/maps before deciding
// whether to call mprotect(), to avoid the syscall when unnecessary. Under
// this game's heavy multi-thread load that check was unreliable both ways
// -- "fail open" (assume writable) on a transient fopen failure skipped the
// real fix entirely, and even after switching to "fail closed" the
// map-file read could still race with concurrent allocations elsewhere in
// the process and misreport. mprotect() itself is cheap and idempotent, so
// just call it unconditionally every time instead of trying to predict
// whether it's needed -- removes the fragile check from the critical path.
static bool HandPatchEnsureWritable(void* host_addr, size_t len) {
	// HAND PATCH (neutered): the mprotect() force-writable workaround is
	// obsolete -- the runtime's AccessViolationCallback now recovers stale
	// guest-writable/host-readonly pages for ALL heap types, and doing it
	// here via raw mprotect stripped the GPU write-watch protection, which
	// broke texture upload tracking (black menu/UI). Keep the symbol so
	// call sites compile; it must do nothing.
	return simpsons::native_gpu_memory::EnsureCommandStorageWritable(host_addr, len);
}

